\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

\paragraph{Base model and evaluation.}
We evaluate on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B~\citep{deepseek2025r1}, a 7B-parameter reasoning model built on the Qwen2.5 architecture~\citep{qwen2.5} with GQA~\citep{ainslie2023gqa} (architecture details in Section~\ref{sec:prelim}).
Our primary benchmark is GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}, a set of 1{,}319 grade-school math problems requiring multi-step arithmetic reasoning.
We report exact-match accuracy: the final numerical answer is extracted from the model's generation and compared against the ground truth.
For rapid iteration during development, we use a 100-problem subset ($N{=}100$); final results use the full test set ($N{=}1{,}319$).
The original unpruned model achieves 90.83\% on GSM8K (greedy decoding).

\paragraph{Pruning methods.}
We compare four pruning criteria at 20\% structured sparsity, all removing the same number of MLP channel groups (200 of 784 across 28 layers, ${\sim}$1.45B parameters) with identical per-layer capacity floors (${\ge}$40\% retention per layer):
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{CKA-only}: structures ranked by functional overlap $F(h)$ alone (Eq.~\ref{eq:overlap}); the most redundant groups are removed regardless of gradient importance.
    \item \textbf{Random}: uniform random selection among eligible groups.
    \item \textbf{Standard}: Taylor-expansion importance $I(h)$ alone (Eq.~\ref{eq:importance}); the least important groups are removed, following LLM-Pruner~\citep{llmpruner2023}.
    \item \textbf{\rasp{}} ($\alpha{=}1.0$): the composite score $S(h) = I(h) \times (1 - F(h))^\alpha$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:score}), removing structures that are both low-importance and functionally redundant.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Recovery pipeline and hardware.}
All four methods undergo matched two-stage recovery.
Stage~1 (SFT warmup): 2 epochs on 7{,}473 GSM8K chain-of-thought examples with teacher forcing (37 minutes), restoring EOS generation and basic format compliance.
Stage~2 (GRPO): Group Relative Policy Optimization~\citep{shao2024grpo} with learning rate $1{\times}10^{-6}$, maximum completion length 1{,}024 tokens, KL penalty coefficient $\beta{=}0.01$, sampling temperature 0.4, DAPO-style clipped loss, and binary reward ($r{=}1$ if correct, $r{=}0$ otherwise), trained for 100 steps on 1{,}000 GSM8K training problems.
All methods use identical GRPO configuration with \texttt{max\_completion\_length}{$=$}1{,}024, except Random which uses the TRL default of 512 tokens (Table~\ref{tab:hyperparams}), making it a conservative comparison point.
Seeds vary at the GRPO stage; pruning calibration and SFT use identical configurations across all methods.
We repeat GRPO training with 3 random seeds for CKA-only, Standard, and Random (Table~\ref{tab:decoupling}).
CKA-only achieves 64.39\% $\pm$ 0.19\,pp (std across seeds), Standard 59.84\% $\pm$ 0.38\,pp; the 4.55\,pp gap is robust across seeds (Welch's $t$-test, $p{=}0.0009$, Cohen's $d{=}11.1$; 95\% CI for mean difference: $[3.62, 5.47]$\,pp), confirming that recovery capacity differences are structural rather than stochastic.
All experiments run on 2$\times$ NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (94\,GB VRAM each); pruning variants are trained in parallel on separate GPUs under identical wall-clock conditions.


\subsection{Recovery Capacity Spectrum}
\label{sec:recovery_gradient}

\input{tables/tab1_recovery_gradient}

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \begin{minipage}[t]{0.38\textwidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig2_reward_trajectories.pdf}
        \centerline{\small (a) Reward trajectories}
    \end{minipage}
    \hfill
    \begin{minipage}[t]{0.58\textwidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/paper/fig3_collapse_dynamics.pdf}
        \centerline{\small (b) $f_0$ and gradient norm diagnostics}
    \end{minipage}
    \caption{GRPO training dynamics for four pruning criteria (100 steps, $\beta{=}0.01$, $T{=}0.4$). (a)~Reward: CKA-only reaches 0.354 peak; Random oscillates (0.14--0.19); Standard peaks at 0.081; \rasp{} collapses to 0.010. (b)~Left: $f_0$ (zero-variance batch fraction)---CKA-only stays below 0.35 while Standard and \rasp{} remain in the collapse zone ($f_0 > 0.5$). Right: gradient norm---\rasp{} norms near zero throughout; Standard shows a transient minimum at step 90.}
    \label{fig:training_dynamics}
\end{figure*}

Under GRPO training, the four pruning criteria produce a clear recovery capacity spectrum: CKA-only $\gg$ Random $\gg$ Standard $>$ \rasp{} (Table~\ref{tab:recovery_gradient}, Figure~\ref{fig:training_dynamics}).
The pruning criterion, not training configuration, determines whether the model learns, stagnates, or collapses.

CKA-only is the only method that produces active learning: peak reward 0.354 with $f_0$ as low as 0.125 (Figure~\ref{fig:training_dynamics}b).
Random stagnates (reward 0.14--0.19, $f_0 \approx 0.33$); Standard exhibits borderline collapse ($f_0 \geq 0.50$, peak reward 0.081); \rasp{} collapses fully ($f_0 \geq 0.675$, reward declining to 0.010).
The 5.8$\times$ peak reward gap between CKA-only and \rasp{} arises entirely from which structures were removed.


\subsection{Reward-Accuracy Decoupling}
\label{sec:decoupling}

\input{tables/tab2_decoupling}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig_sft_grpo_scatter.pdf}
    \caption{SFT vs GRPO accuracy across all pruning criteria and sparsity levels on GSM8K ($N{=}1{,}319$). Points near the $y{=}x$ line indicate reward-accuracy decoupling: despite 4.4$\times$ peak reward gaps (Figure~\ref{fig:training_dynamics}a), GRPO barely moves accuracy ($|\Delta| \leq 1$\,pp for all fine-grained methods at 20--25\%).}
    \label{fig:sft_grpo_scatter}
\end{figure}

Despite the 4.4$\times$ peak reward gap, GRPO barely moves accuracy (Figure~\ref{fig:sft_grpo_scatter}): all fine-grained methods at 20\% sparsity change by $|\Delta| < 1$\,pp (CKA-only $+$0.48, Standard $+$0.55, Random $-$0.53, \rasp{} $-$0.61\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:decoupling}).
We term this \emph{reward-accuracy decoupling}: reward trajectory reflects the model's capacity to learn from RL signal, not downstream accuracy.
At 20\% sparsity, the four fine-grained criteria show mean $|\Delta| {=} 0.54$\,pp (max $0.61$\,pp); across the full $\alpha$ dose-response spectrum, $|\Delta|$ remains below $1.14$\,pp.

The degree of decoupling varies with sparsity: at 15\%, GRPO slightly amplifies the CKA-only advantage (SFT gap 5.69\,pp $\to$ GRPO gap 7.20\,pp); at 20\%, GRPO adds nothing (gap preserved at 4.55\,pp); at 25\%, Standard's peak reward collapses to 0.008 yet accuracy still changes by only $+$0.99\,pp.
Across all four fine-grained criteria at 20\% and 25\%, $|\Delta| < 1$\,pp without exception on GSM8K (Tables~\ref{tab:decoupling},~\ref{tab:cross_sparsity}); dose-response $\alpha$ variations show slightly larger but still small differences (max $1.14$\,pp at $\alpha{=}2.0$; Table~\ref{tab:alpha}; MATH-500 differences within statistical noise, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_math500}).

These results are robust across seeds (0.37\,pp range; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_multiseed}) and extended training (300 steps, accuracy unchanged; \S\ref{sec:extended_analysis}).
The endpoints of the spectrum generalize beyond GSM8K: on MATH-500~\citep{hendrycks2021math}, CKA-only and Standard both rank above \rasp{} and Random in SFT accuracy (26.8\%, 23.4\% vs.\ 21.2\%, 14.4\%), though the CKA-only--Standard gap narrows (3.4\,pp vs.\ 4.6\,pp on GSM8K; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_math500})---possibly because MATH-500's higher difficulty compresses all pruned methods toward a floor.
On MBPP~\citep{austin2021mbpp} all fine-grained GRPO $|\Delta| < 2$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_mbpp}).
\label{sec:ood}
ShortGPT~\citep{shortgpt2024} depth pruning is the sole exception: its GRPO delta is task-dependent ($-$1.36\,pp on GSM8K, $+$4.01\,pp on MBPP; Tables~\ref{tab:decoupling},~\ref{tab:mbpp}), unlike fine-grained methods ($|\Delta| \leq 1.34$\,pp).


\subsection{Additional Results}
\label{sec:additional_results}

\paragraph{Post-SFT accuracy does not predict GRPO outcome.}
\label{sec:sft_gradient}
Under SFT alone, CKA-only (63.91\%) $>$ \rasp{} (61.49\%) $>$ Standard (59.29\%) $>$ Random (51.25\%), yet under GRPO the \rasp{}/Standard ordering reverses and Random outperforms both in peak reward.
The spectrum is driven by structural properties of the pruned architecture, not post-SFT accuracy.

\paragraph{Sparsity limits and dose-response.}
\label{sec:sparsity_cliff}
All methods collapse at 30\% sparsity, validating 20\% as the operating point (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sparsity_cliff}).
Pruning reduces parameters by 19\% and memory by ${\sim}$28\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_inference}).
On GSM8K, the CKA coefficient $\alpha$ exhibits an inverted-U pattern in recovery accuracy across $\{0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0\}$, peaking at $\alpha{=}2.0$ (SFT 64.97\%, GRPO 66.11\%) with $|\Delta| \leq 1.14$\,pp at every point; $\alpha{=}1.0$ collapses (peak reward 0.061) while $\alpha{=}4.0$ recovers to 62.77\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_alpha}).\footnote{$\alpha{=}1.0$ uses \texttt{max\_completion\_length}{$=$}1{,}024; other $\alpha$ values use the TRL default of 512. Since $\alpha{=}1.0$ is the curve minimum, this makes the inverted-U a conservative estimate.}


\subsection{Extended Analysis}
\label{sec:extended_analysis}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig4_cka_heatmap.pdf}
    \caption{Layer-wise CKA similarity between dense and pruned representations (28 layers). CKA-only diverges most from dense ($\mu{=}0.62$) yet recovers best; Standard ($\mu{=}0.90$), Random ($\mu{=}0.92$), and \rasp{} ($\mu{=}0.88$) preserve dense fidelity but collapse under RL.}
    \label{fig:cka_heatmap}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Representational diversity.}
CKA similarity between dense and pruned representations (Figure~\ref{fig:cka_heatmap}) shows CKA-only diverges most from the dense model ($\mu{=}0.62$), while Standard ($\mu{=}0.90$), Random ($\mu{=}0.92$), and \rasp{} ($\mu{=}0.88$) cluster near 1.0.
Preserving dense fidelity through gradient importance appears to retain redundant representations at the expense of the diversity that GRPO exploration requires.
CKA-only concentrates pruning in early layers (L0--9, 72.5\% of removed groups) while Standard prunes uniformly, removing late-layer structures that are representationally unique (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_overlap}).
Gradient flow analysis corroborates this: CKA-only models exhibit 2.89$\times$ mean gradient magnitude versus Standard across 55/56 layer groups ($p{<}0.05$, Wilcoxon; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_gradient}), consistent with diversity preservation enabling stronger RL training signals.

